/*!
    \page qtbridges-rust-index.html
    \title Qt Bridges - Rust
    \brief How to get started with Qt Bridges using Rust.

    \section1 Introduction

    This library enables building modern Qt Quick user interfaces with a Rust
    backend. It allows you to run
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qmlreference.html}{QML} code and expose Rust data
    structures directly to the
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qqmlengine.html}{QML Engine}, combining a
    declarative UI with Rust-based business logic.

    Qt itself is written in C++, and Qt Bridge builds on
    \l {https://cxx.rs/}{CXX} to access the required Qt interfaces. As a user,
    you do not need to write any C++ code. Instead, Rust structs and data
    can be exposed to QML using attribute macros provided by the library.

    If your project requires mixing Rust and C++ code, using Qt Widgets, or
    accessing Qt modules that only provide a C++ API, consider using
    \l {https://github.com/KDAB/cxx-qt}{CXX-Qt} instead.

    Internally, the library relies on Qt concepts such as
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qobject.html}{QObjects},
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/properties.html}{properties},
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/signalsandslots.html}{signals and slots},
    and the \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/model-view-programming.html}{Model/View
    architecture}. While these are exposed through a Rust-friendly API,
    familiarity with these Qt concepts will help you get the
    most out of building UIs with Qt Quick.

    \section1 Project structure

    This repos consists both of Qt Bridge itself under \c qtbridge directory
    as well as application examples in \c {apps}. For more detaild information,
    see the \l {https://code.qt.io/cgit/qt/qtbridge-rust.git/about/}{Qt Bridge -
    Rust git repo}.

    \section2

    To build this project, you’ll need:

    \list
        \li \b Rust (stable, version >= 1.87) - Visit
        \l {https://rustup.rs}{rustup.rs} to install
        \li \b {Cargo and rustfmt} (comes with Rust)
        \li \l {Qt installation}{\b {Qt 6 installation}}
        \li \b {C++ toolchain} (compiler, linker etc)
    \endlist

    Optional:
    \list
        \li \b {clang-format} available in build environment
    \endlist

    \section2 Qt installation

    The Qt installation must be present in the system and \c qmake must be in
    the system PATH. There are no special requirements to the Qt installation.
    It can be built from source or downloaded from \l {https://download.qt.io/}
    {download.qt.io}. To ensure \c qmake is available, add the Qt bin directory
    to your PATH, for example:

    Example (Linux):
    \badcode
        export PATH=<PATH_TO_QT>/bin:$PATH
    \endcode

    Example (Windows PowerShell):
    \badcode
        $env:Path = "<PATH_TO_QT>\bin;" + $env:Path
    \endcode

    \section3

    On Linux it might be also required to add the \c {Qt/lib} directory to the
    \c {LD_LIBRARY_PATH}.

    Example:
    \badcode
        export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<PATH_TO_QT>/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
    \endcode

    \section2 Build using cargo

    \badcode
        cargo build
    \endcode

    \section2 Run using cargo

    \badcode
        cargo run --bin <binary_name>
    \endcode

    \section2 Run tests:

    \badcode
        cargo test
    \endcode

    \section2 Environment variables

    The folder containing qmake must be in PATH, for example:

    On Windows:

    \badcode
        set PATH=%PATH%;D:\dev\qt_build\qtbase\bin\
    \endcode

    On Linux:

    \badcode
        export PATH=/home/john_doe/dev/qt_build/qtbase/bin:$PATH
        export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="/home/john_doe/dev/qt_build/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
    \endcode

    \section1 Examples

    \section2 Example code

    Running a QML application is as easy as creating a \c QApp instance and
    loading the respective QML files:

    \badcode
        use qtbridge::QApp;

        fn main() {
            QApp::new()
                .load_qml(include_bytes!("Main.qml"))
                .run();
        }
    \endcode

    A Rust backend can be added with the \c {#[qobject_impl]} and
    \c {#[qml_element]} macros:

    \badcode
        use qtbridge::{qobject_impl, qml_element};

        #[derive(Default)]
        pub struct Backend {
        }

        #[qobject_impl]
        #[qml_element]
        impl Backend {
            #[qslot]
            fn say_hello(&self) {
                println!("Hello World!")
            }
        }
    \endcode

    QML can use the backend as follows:

    \qml
        import QtQuick
        import QtQuick.Controls
        import hello_world

        ApplicationWindow {
            visible: true

            Backend {
                id: backend
            }

            Button {
                anchors.centerIn: parent
                text: "Hello World!"
                onClicked: backend.sayHello()
            }
        }
    \endqml

    \section2 Provided Examples

    There are two examples:

    \list
        \li hello_world
        \li minimal_app
    \endlist

    To run those applications, you can use \c {cargo run} from one of its
    directories.

    There are also the following aliases defined, which can be
    executed from the root dir:

    Hello world example:

    \badcode
        cargo run-hello-world
    \endcode

    Minimal app example:

    \badcode
        cargo run-minimal-app
    \endcode

    \section1 API overview

    \section2 Borrow checker and rules

    Qt Bridges aims to respect the borrowing rules of Rust. All QObjects in Rust
    (implemented with \c {qobject_impl}) are designed to be held in
    \c {Rc<RefCell<_>>} structs. The QML engine also hold Rust objects through
    such references. With this construct, many references can be held and
    borrowed at runtime. Their QML representation can be copied following the
    ordinary JavaScript rules. A Rust object is only borrowed when a slot or
    property defined in Rust is invoked. If QML is unable to borrow the Rust
    object, it will panic. We are currently investigating alternative ways to
    handle this situation more gracefully.

    It is important that you do not hold a reference to any object that is
    accessed from QML when \c {QApp::run()} is executed. For example, the
    following code will fail when the QML engine tries to call into a slot
    defined in Rust:

    \badcode
        let qobject1: Rc<RefCell<Backend>> = Backend::default_with_attached_qobject();
        let qobject2: Rc<RefCell<Backend>> = Backend::default_with_attached_qobject();

        QApp::new()
            .with_initial_properties(& [
                ("rustmodel1", qobject1.borrow().as_qvariant()),
                ("rustmodel2", qobject2.borrow().as_qvariant()),
            ])
            .load_qml(include_bytes!("main.qml"))
            .run();
    \endcode

    Isolating the borrowing into separate calls resolves the issue:

    \badcode
        let qobject1: Rc<RefCell<Backend>> = Backend::default_with_attached_qobject();
        let qobject2: Rc<RefCell<Backend>> = Backend::default_with_attached_qobject();

        let initial_properties = [
            ("rustmodel1", qobject1.borrow().as_qvariant()),
            ("rustmodel2", qobject2.borrow().as_qvariant()),
        ];
        QApp::new()
            .with_initial_properties(&initial_properties)
            .load_qml(include_bytes!("main.qml"))
            .run();
    \endcode

    This is an evolving API and we aim to make it safer in the future.

    \section2 QApp

    \c QApp represents a Qt QML application and acts as the entry point for all
    applications. It allows running QML code and injecting Rust objects into it.

    \section2 qobject_impl

    The \c {#[qobject_impl]} macro allows user-defined Rust structs to
    integrate with Qt and QML by supporting
    \b {signals, slots, and properties}. When a struct is annotated with
    \c {#[qobject_impl]}, the macro analyzes the contents of the struct’s impl
    block and generates the required glue code to connect the struct to Qt’s
    meta-object system.

    Using this macro, a developer can:

    \list
        \li Notify QML about events related to a Rust object (via \b {signals}).
        \li Define \b slots that can connect to signals or be invoked directly
        from QML.
        \li Expose object values to QML as \b {properties}, supporting read and
        optionally write access.
    \endlist

    For more details, see the Rust documentation for \c {#[qobject_impl]}.

    \section2 qml_element

    The \c {#[qml_element]} macro makes Rust structures instantiable from QML.
    It has to be used together with \c {#[qobject_impl]}. This lets you to
    write idomatic QML code.

    \section2 QVec

    \c QVec provides an API similar to Rust’s standard
    \l {https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html}{Vec}.
    In addition, it is recognized by QML as a model and can be used with UI
    elements such as
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qml-qtquick-listview.html}{ListView},
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qml-qtquick-repeater.html}{Repeater}, and other
    components that require a model.

    Currently, \c QVec can only be exposed to QML through the initial properties
    of the QML engine.

    \section2 Resource system

    The best way to get artifacts, such as icons, into a QML application is
    through the
    \l {https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/resources.html}{Qt Resource System}. To inject
    data into the Qt Resource System, you can use the macro
    \c {include_bytes_qml!} or the function \c {qresource::register_bytes}.

    \section1 Future Plans

    \list
        \li Enable interoperability with \l
        {https://crates.io/crates/cxx-qt}{CXX-Qt}.
        \li Enable interoperability with other libraries such as
        \l {https://tokio.rs/}{tokio}.
        \li Enable QObjects as properties (complex properties).
        \li Currently we provide \c QVec as an abstraction for
        \c {QAbstractItemModel}. We aim to provide a trait based API with more
        flexibility in the future.
        \li The minimal_app shows an API that resembles CXX-Qt. This will be
        replaced with an API that looks more native.
        \li Merge macros \c {qobject_impl} and \c {qml_element}.
        \li Remove all C++ Qt classes from the API: Currently, some operations
        require the usage of QVariant, QModelIndex and there likes. We are
        working on an API that does not require those.
        \li Provide Qt dependencies as a crate to enable a streamlined
        installation through cargo.
        \li Remove the dependency on Qt binaries like qmake and maybe a C++
        tool chain.
        \li Extend IDE support, in particular for VS Code. Enable the QML
        language server to understand types generated in Rust.
    \endlist

